{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Deploy the supplied stdio handler on node1 TCP port 9097 through inetd or xinetd, with one handler process per connection.","status":"satisfied","summary":"OpenBSD inetd from the Xenial repositories was installed, enabled, and configured with a `nowait` entry for the supplied handler. Direct observations show inetd listening on TCP 9097 and six held connections mapped to six distinct handler processes.","evidence_ids":["cmd-1e6e5499e5604414","cmd-71f1f6e1b08c47f6","cmd-2106d3d64db64e3a","cmd-dcb9797e62b3402e","cmd-2103e72b42b34d9a"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"From node2, PING followed by a newline returns PONG, while other lines return ERROR.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Sequential tests covered PING, invalid, and empty requests; concurrent tests produced the expected PONG and ERROR responses. The behavior was also directly reconfirmed after both node restarts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-2dd8c50003aa47e2","cmd-08ac5df0fbc84c8e","cmd-4d64d80eacf54255"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Multiple sequential and concurrent clients finish, with no handler processes left behind.","status":"satisfied","summary":"All 16 concurrent clients in the initial test and all 10 in the final test completed. A held-connection test directly showed six simultaneous handler PIDs, all six clients subsequently completed, and handler counts returned to zero both before and after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-2dd8c50003aa47e2","cmd-dcb9797e62b3402e","cmd-92da0bf086414dfa","cmd-885936b4c2174cee","cmd-312938a22419418e","cmd-4d64d80eacf54255","cmd-2103e72b42b34d9a"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Preserve the protocol notes in /srv/legacy2/inetd-request-activation.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The protocol note retained its original contents and baseline SHA-256 value on both nodes through deployment and restarts.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","seeded-state-node2","cmd-42bf82d5f0324eec","cmd-312938a22419418e","cmd-08ac5df0fbc84c8e","cmd-2103e72b42b34d9a"]},{"id":"R5","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel, using software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes remained on Ubuntu 16.04.7 with kernel 4.4.0-210-generic. The installed OpenBSD inetd package was the Xenial i386 repository version, with no package upgrades reported.","evidence_ids":["cmd-1e6e5499e5604414","cmd-71f1f6e1b08c47f6","cmd-4d64d80eacf54255","cmd-2103e72b42b34d9a","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform"]}],"confidence":0.97,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.94,"summary":"The task-related mutations—installing inetd, adding its service entries, enabling it, and running bounded client/restart tests—had clear roles. Package health remained sound and no failed services were observed. The restart protocol was strong for both nodes: pre-restart health and boot IDs, acknowledgements, changed post-restart IDs, and recovered data and behavior were captured and agree with the fixed lifecycle identities. The only unwanted retained mutation was `/tmp/final9097` with ten output files on node2, created for final evidence by cmd-4d64d80eacf54255 and not cleaned. Earlier held-client temporary files were testing prerequisites and were absent from the final snapshot. No other unnecessary mutations are evident.","evidence_ids":["cmd-4d64d80eacf54255","cmd-09db5e473d67437e","cmd-bc6df1597e4e4a55","cmd-312938a22419418e","cmd-9c3287503dd64429","cmd-08ac5df0fbc84c8e","global:after_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity","global:after_prepare:node2:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node2:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct end-to-end evidence. The service correctly provides one inetd-spawned handler per connection, handles sequential and concurrent node2 clients, leaves no handler processes behind, preserves the supplied files, and retains the legacy OS and kernel. Both managed nodes also have complete, cross-checked restart and recovery evidence. The sole hygiene issue is a small set of retained temporary final-test outputs on node2.","limitations":["The fixed after-executor listening-socket snapshot is empty and therefore does not independently corroborate TCP 9097, although multiple direct executor observations immediately before collection show the listener and successful remote traffic.","The lifecycle collector does not inventory inetd configuration or the task directory; persistence and file integrity therefore rely on the direct post-restart executor observations.","The pre-restart node1 process search in cmd-09db5e473d67437e matched its own command text, but later exact process counts and socket-to-PID observations provide unambiguous evidence."]}
